var e=[{id:`rc-p01`,title:`The Case for Judicial Minimalism`,domain:`law`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`When appellate judges confront a difficult constitutional question, they face a choice that is rarely named in opinions yet shapes nearly every one of them: how broadly should the court rule? One influential answer, often called judicial minimalism, holds that judges should decide no more than the case before them requires, and should do so on grounds that are as shallow as possible — grounds that avoid committing the legal system to a grand theory. A minimalist opinion resolves the dispute, gives reasons sufficient to justify the result, and leaves larger questions for another day.`,`The argument for this restraint rests on three related claims. First, judges deciding under conditions of moral and empirical uncertainty are prone to error, and narrow rulings limit the damage when they err. A sweeping opinion that misreads the Constitution binds thousands of future cases; a narrow one can be corrected with less wreckage. Second, minimalism is said to respect democratic self-government: by leaving contested questions open, courts return them to legislatures and citizens, where they arguably belong. Third, minimalists contend that restraint protects the judiciary’s own legitimacy. Courts that issue modest, case-bound decisions appear less like a rival legislature and more like the impartial tribunal the public expects them to be.`,`Critics of minimalism — sometimes called maximalists — answer that clarity is itself a judicial virtue, and that minimalism systematically undersupplies it. A decision that resolves one dispute while leaving the governing principle obscure does not so much settle the law as postpone the fight, they argue, inviting waves of follow-on litigation as lower courts and litigants struggle to extract guidance from an intentionally thin opinion. Where a broad ruling would give citizens and officials a rule they can plan around, a minimalist ruling gives them a result and a shrug. Worse, critics add, shallow reasoning can mask disagreement rather than resolve it: judges who cannot agree on first principles paper over the split with a narrow holding, producing opinions whose logic no one — not even their authors — can fully defend.`,`The historical record offers ammunition to both camps, and honest partisans on each side concede the point. Minimalist landmarks have sometimes generated years of costly confusion as lower courts guessed at the principle the higher court declined to state. But the maximalist alternative has its own embarrassments: several of the most confidently reasoned, theory-laden opinions in the canon are now taught mainly as cautionary tales about judges who mistook the moment’s certainties for timeless truths. A narrow decision can usually be revisited; a broad one, once woven into doctrine, resists correction.`,`The deepest unresolved question is whether minimalism is best understood as a general theory of judging or merely as a situational tactic. As a universal prescription it seems implausible — some disputes, such as those requiring lower courts to administer a workable standard across thousands of cases, genuinely demand breadth. As a rebuttable default, however, the minimalist impulse has considerable force: decide the case at hand, say only what must be said, and let the broader edifice of doctrine be built incrementally, one decided controversy at a time. What remains untested is whether that counsel survives the cases that tempt judges most — those in which saying less feels, to the deciding court, like an abdication rather than a discipline.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p01-q1`,`rc-p01-q2`,`rc-p01-q3`,`rc-p01-q4`,`rc-p01-q5`,`rc-p01-q6`,`rc-p01-q7`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p02`,title:`Plea Bargaining on Trial`,domain:`law`,comparative:!1,topicCluster:`plea-bargaining`,parts:[{paragraphs:[`More than nine out of ten criminal convictions in the United States result not from trials but from guilty pleas, most of them negotiated. This system — in which prosecutors offer concessions such as reduced charges or sentencing recommendations in exchange for a defendant’s waiver of trial rights — is so entrenched that it is easy to forget it was once regarded as improper. Through much of the nineteenth century, American courts treated plea bargaining as a sordid practice to be discouraged; judges routinely refused to honor prosecutorial promises and warned that justice should not be bartered. Only in the twentieth century, as urban caseloads swelled beyond anything a trial-centered system could absorb, did the practice move from the shadows into the open, culminating in decisions such as Brady v. United States (1970), in which the Supreme Court declared negotiated pleas not merely tolerable but an essential component of the administration of justice.`,`Defenders of the system argue that plea bargaining serves the interests of everyone involved. Defendants who plead guilty typically receive lighter sentences than they would risk at trial, avoid the expense and anxiety of prolonged proceedings, and gain a measure of control over an otherwise uncertain outcome. Prosecutors conserve scarce resources for the cases that genuinely require adjudication. And the public benefits from a system that resolves cases swiftly rather than collapsing under its own weight: without bargaining, the courts would face a backlog so severe that the constitutional promise of a speedy trial would become a dead letter.`,`Critics respond that these mutual advantages mask a profound imbalance of power. The central objection is the so-called trial penalty — the gap between the sentence offered in exchange for a plea and the far harsher sentence threatened if the defendant insists on trial and loses. When that gap grows large enough, critics argue, the defendant’s choice ceases to be meaningful: pleading guilty becomes less an admission than a rational surrender to coercion. Empirical research has sharpened the worry. Studies of exonerations have found that a disturbing share of wrongly convicted defendants — including some later cleared by DNA evidence — originally pleaded guilty, suggesting that innocent people do, under pressure, confess to crimes they did not commit. Moreover, the burdens of the system fall unevenly: defendants who cannot afford vigorous counsel or pretrial release are least able to call the prosecution’s bluff, and available data indicate that these disadvantages track race and class.`,`Proponents counter that the abuses critics document are failures of regulation, not of bargaining itself. No market functions well without rules, they observe, and the plea market is among the least regulated in American law: prosecutors enjoy nearly unchecked discretion over charging and offers, defense counsel is often underfunded, and judges — who in the federal system may not even participate in negotiations — typically learn the terms only at the moment of approval. The proper response, on this view, is not to abolish a practice on which the system depends but to civilize it: broader pretrial discovery so defendants can assess the evidence against them, meaningful judicial oversight of proposed deals, and limits on the most extreme trial penalties.`,`What both sides increasingly accept is that abolition is a fantasy. No legislature will fund the tenfold expansion of courts, prosecutors, and defenders that a trial for every defendant would require, and few defendants would thank reformers for forcing them to gamble at trial rather than accept a favorable offer. The live question is therefore narrower but no less consequential: which safeguards can preserve bargaining’s efficiency while curbing its coercive edge? Proposals range from requiring prosecutors to disclose exculpatory evidence before a plea to capping the differential between plea offers and post-trial sentences. None commands consensus. For practitioners, the consequence is practical: the bargain’s honesty will be decided less by the abolition debate than by the slow accretion of safeguards — disclosure rules, sentencing caps — each tested against the coercion it was meant to curb.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p02-q1`,`rc-p02-q2`,`rc-p02-q3`,`rc-p02-q4`,`rc-p02-q5`,`rc-p02-q6`,`rc-p02-q7`],estimatedMinutes:10},{id:`rc-p03`,title:`Adverse Possession: An Ancient Doctrine Defended`,domain:`law`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Few doctrines of property law strike the layperson as more paradoxical than adverse possession: the rule that a trespasser who occupies another’s land openly, continuously, and without permission for a prescribed number of years can emerge as its lawful owner. To the skeptic, the doctrine appears to reward wrongdoing — to convert theft, patiently practiced, into title. Yet adverse possession is among the oldest principles in Western law. Roman jurists recognized usucapio, by which long possession ripened into ownership; English limitation statutes of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries barred stale claims to land; and American courts enthusiastically adopted the doctrine, finding it well suited to a frontier society in which formal title records were often chaotic and productive settlement was a public good.`,`The traditional justifications for the doctrine are principally economic and evidentiary. Economically, adverse possession is said to move land toward its most productive user: an owner who sleeps on their rights for decades while another farms, builds on, or maintains the parcel has, by revealed preference, placed little value on it. Evidentiary concerns reinforce the point. Memories fade, witnesses die, and documents are lost; after enough time, adjudicating who truly owned a parcel generations ago becomes guesswork. A limitations period forces owners to assert claims while proof is fresh and quiets titles that would otherwise remain clouded indefinitely. Together, these rationales present adverse possession not as a prize for trespassers but as a penalty for neglect — a spur to vigilant ownership.`,`The moral critique of the doctrine has nevertheless gained force, and its strongest version targets the doctrine’s fit with modern conditions. The frontier rationale has expired: contemporary recording systems make title searches routine, so the chaos that once justified sweeping away stale claims has largely been tamed. Meanwhile, the doctrine’s application can look less like productive stewardship than opportunism — the deliberate occupation of a neighbor’s unused strip of land in hopes of eventually claiming it. Critics argue that when the law’s evidentiary and economic justifications weaken, what remains is a rule that teaches citizens that persistent boundary-pushing pays, corroding respect for property rights it is supposed to serve.`,`Defenders respond that the critique overstates both the doctrine’s harshness and its obsolescence. Adverse possession, as actually applied, is hedged with requirements that filter out casual opportunism: possession must be open and notorious rather than secretive, continuous for the full statutory period, hostile to the true owner’s rights, and in many jurisdictions accompanied by payment of property taxes or held under a good-faith but mistaken claim of title. These elements mean the successful claimant is typically not a calculating invader but a neighbor who fenced, farmed, or built in the honest belief the land was theirs. And the evidentiary rationale, defenders insist, has not vanished: boundary disputes still turn on decades-old surveys and vanished landmarks, and some mechanism for settling them remains necessary.`,`The most plausible resolution, and the direction several states have already taken, is reform rather than repeal. Legislatures have lengthened limitations periods, required good faith or color of title, and exempted certain categories of land — such as government-owned parcels — from the doctrine’s reach. Such measures preserve what is valuable in adverse possession — its quieting of ancient disputes and its reward of productive, long-standing use — while blunting the charge that it licenses land theft. The reform strategy will be testable within a generation: if the narrowed doctrine still quiets ancient disputes, the pruners were right; if old claims start resurfacing in court, the repealers will get their second hearing.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p03-q1`,`rc-p03-q2`,`rc-p03-q3`,`rc-p03-q4`,`rc-p03-q5`,`rc-p03-q6`,`rc-p03-q7`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p04`,title:`Reading Vermeer's Interiors`,domain:`humanities`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Few painters reward sustained looking the way Johannes Vermeer does. A typical canvas shows a sunlit domestic interior: a woman reading a letter, a maid pouring milk, a map hanging on a plastered wall. The scenes are rendered with an almost photographic attention to the behavior of light on surfaces — the glint of a brass nail, the soft bloom of bread crust, the blue of a bodice deepened by shadow. For much of the twentieth century, art historians treated these paintings primarily as technical achievements and windows onto seventeenth-century Dutch daily life. More recently, however, a vigorous debate has arisen over whether Vermeer’s quiet rooms are, beneath their calm surfaces, moral arguments in paint.`,`The moralizing interpretation begins from an observation about the world in which Vermeer worked. The Dutch Republic of the 1650s and 1660s was saturated with emblem books and proverbs that assigned moral meanings to everyday objects: a balance could signify divine judgment or merely the weighing of pearls; a letter might represent faithful love or illicit correspondence; an overturned chair could hint at disorder, domestic or spiritual. Scholars in this camp argue that Vermeer’s original viewers, steeped in this visual vocabulary, would have read his interiors as vanitas meditations — reminders of virtue, temperance, and the perils of idleness. On this reading, the famous milkmaid is not simply pouring milk but embodying the virtue of diligent domestic labor, and the woman with the balance is being invited, along with the viewer, to weigh her soul.`,`Skeptics of the moralizing reading do not deny that seventeenth-century viewers knew their emblems; they deny that Vermeer necessarily deployed them. Their central objection is methodological. Once the interpreter assumes that every object is a symbol, the method becomes unfalsifiable: a map on the wall can mean worldly ambition or honest cartography, a pearl earring vanity or simply wealth, depending on what the thesis requires. The skeptics point out that many of the same objects appear in the work of Vermeer’s Delft contemporaries — painters of modest reputation whose pictures no one proposes to read as theology — which suggests the objects may reflect the standard props of a shared studio practice rather than a coded moral program. To treat the paintings as puzzles to be decoded, they argue, is to impose a literary model of meaning on images whose power may lie precisely in their resistance to paraphrase.`,`Each side can claim telling evidence, and the debate has been productive precisely because neither can land a decisive blow. Technical studies of Vermeer’s working methods have shown that he adjusted compositions with extraordinary care — moving a map, altering a chair’s position — which the moralizers take as proof of deliberation about meaning, and the skeptics take as proof of deliberation about pictorial harmony. Archival research has meanwhile complicated the picture: inventories of Delft households show that the objects Vermeer painted were genuinely common possessions, which cuts both ways, since a symbol must be familiar to be legible.`,`The emerging consensus, if it can be called that, is a chastened middle position. Vermeer surely knew the emblematic tradition and may have expected some viewers to catch moral overtones; but the paintings refuse to resolve into allegory, and their greatness arguably depends on that refusal. The irony is that the emblematic readings now look like the proverb-hunting the pictures resist, while the skeptics have restored the freer experience of simply looking. Vermeer’s interiors endure because they hold the descriptive and the suggestive in balance — a balance four centuries of commentary have not managed to tip.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p04-q1`,`rc-p04-q3`,`rc-p04-q4`,`rc-p04-q5`,`rc-p04-q6`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p05`,title:`The Unreliable Narrator, Reconsidered`,domain:`humanities`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`In 1961 the literary critic Wayne Booth gave a name to a phenomenon readers had long sensed without quite naming: the unreliable narrator, a fictional speaker whose account of events the reader is meant to doubt. Booth’s prototype was the governess of Henry James’s 1898 novella The Turn of the Screw, whose chilling tale of haunted children may be a ghost story, a case study in self-deception, or both. The concept proved irresistibly useful. Teachers acquired a vocabulary for discussing narrative technique, critics a framework for analyzing irony, and generations of students a pleasantly subversive lens: the narrator, that trusted guide, might be lying — or worse, might not know they are lying.`,`Yet the very success of the concept has provoked a counter-movement among narratologists who argue that "unreliable narration" misdescribes what actually happens when we read. Their central claim is that unreliability is not a property of the narrator at all but a judgment performed by the reader. A text, on this view, does not come stamped "unreliable"; rather, readers infer unreliability when a narrator’s claims clash with other signals — inconsistencies in the account, contradictions with what other characters report, or violations of the reader’s background expectations about how the world works. The governess seems unreliable not because James labeled her so but because her story strains against details she herself provides: the children’s odd composure, the conveniently unverifiable apparitions, her own admitted agitation.`,`This reader-centered account gains plausibility from a striking empirical observation: readers disagree, often sharply, about which narrators are unreliable and in what way. One reader’s self-deceived governess is another’s heroic protector of children; whole critical industries have been built on each side. If unreliability were a stable textual property, like the number of chapters, such persistent disagreement would be puzzling. Its defenders reply that disagreement reflects differences in readers’ background assumptions rather than indeterminacy in the text — but that concession gives away much of the game, since it admits that the "unreliability" resides in the interaction between text and reader, not in the narrator alone.`,`Traditionalists have not surrendered the concept, and their best defense draws a distinction the critics are said to blur. They concede that readers do the inferring but insist that authors engineer the inference: James plants the governess’s inconsistencies with the evident intention that competent readers notice them. On this view, calling a narrator unreliable is shorthand for a claim about authorial design — that the text was constructed to cue doubt — and the reader’s judgment is simply the successful reception of that design. The disagreement among readers, traditionalists add, is a feature rather than a bug: great fiction sustains multiple coherent readings, and the governess can be both a victim of suggestion and a teller of truths, depending on which cues the reader weights most heavily.`,`Where the debate lands matters beyond the seminar room, because the concept shapes how fiction is taught. An approach that treats unreliability as a label to be affixed — "identify the unreliable narrator" — trains students to hunt for a trick. An approach that treats it as an inference to be argued — "what evidence supports doubting this account, and what would have to be true for the doubt to be misplaced?" — trains them in something more valuable: the weighing of evidence, the articulation of reasons, and the humility of recognizing that even careful readers can disagree. Whatever the narratologists finally decide about where unreliability lives, the classroom has already rendered its verdict on which version teaches better.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p05-q1`,`rc-p05-q2`,`rc-p05-q3`,`rc-p05-q4`,`rc-p05-q5`,`rc-p05-q6`,`rc-p05-q7`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p06`,title:`How Should Historians Explain Change?`,domain:`humanities`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Every history book answers, implicitly or explicitly, one of the discipline’s oldest questions: what kind of thing is a historical explanation? For much of the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, the dominant answer was narrative. Historians told the story of how one thing led to another, and explanation consisted in the artful arrangement of events — causes rendered as episodes in a plot, with human decisions at the center. The fall of Rome, in this tradition, was the story of weak emperors, invading armies, and fateful battles; to explain was to narrate well.`,`The most formidable challenge to this narrative orthodoxy came from the French Annales school, founded in 1929 around the journal Annales d’histoire économique et sociale. The Annalistes argued that event-centered history mistook the foam on the waves for the tide. What truly shaped human societies, they contended, were deep, slow-moving structures: geography and climate, demographic regimes, patterns of trade and technology, the mental frameworks through which whole cultures perceived the world. Fernand Braudel’s monumental study of the Mediterranean in the age of Philip II famously relegated kings and battles to the book’s final section, devoting the bulk of his analysis to mountains, seas, and grain prices. Explanation, for the Annalistes, meant identifying the structural constraints within which events — and individuals — merely played out their assigned roles.`,`The structuralist program yielded genuine insights, but its critics identified a persistent difficulty: structures explain continuity far better than they explain change. If geography and demography determine the course of history, why did the Mediterranean world of 1600 differ so dramatically from that of 1400, when the mountains had not moved and the harvests followed familiar rhythms? Narrative historians seized on this weakness, arguing that structures set the stage but human agency writes the play — that without decisions, contingencies, and the occasional blunder, history becomes a machine without operators. Some went further, charging that structural history, for all its scientific ambition, smuggled its own narrative in through the back door, since selecting which structures matter is itself an interpretive, story-shaping act.`,`Out of this standoff emerged a third approach that has quietly become one of the field’s most fertile: microhistory. Practitioners of microhistory take an apparently insignificant episode — a heresy trial in a Pyrenean village, the worldview of a sixteenth-century miller reconstructed from Inquisition records — and use it as a lens onto the larger structures that the Annalistes described. The method’s wager is that intensive scrutiny of the small scale can reveal how structures were actually experienced, negotiated, and sometimes subverted by the people living inside them. The miller’s cosmology turns out to be neither a mere reflection of peasant culture nor an inexplicable personal invention but a creative recombination of elite and popular ideas — evidence that structures constrain without determining.`,`The debate among narrative, structural, and microhistorical approaches has no umpire, and perhaps that is as it should be. Each answers a different version of the explanation question: narrative history explains how we got from one event to the next; structural history explains why the range of possible outcomes was narrower than contemporaries imagined; microhistory explains how large forces felt from the inside. The ecumenical position carries its own methodological risk: invoking all three scales can become a way of avoiding the hard choice among them, since a history that explains everything at every scale may end up constraining nothing at any. The historian’s task, on this ecumenical view, is not to choose the one true scale of explanation but to match the scale to the question being asked.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p06-q1`,`rc-p06-q2`,`rc-p06-q3`,`rc-p06-q4`,`rc-p06-q5`,`rc-p06-q6`,`rc-p06-q7`,`rc-p06-q8`],estimatedMinutes:10},{id:`rc-p07`,title:`Nudges and the Limits of Libertarian Paternalism`,domain:`social-science`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Human beings, the behavioral economists tell us, are not the rational calculators of textbook economics. We procrastinate, we follow the crowd, we stick with default options out of inertia, and we make systematically different choices depending on how alternatives are framed. From this observation, Richard Thaler and Cass Sunstein drew a striking policy prescription: since the design of any choice environment inevitably influences decisions — there is no neutral way to present options — policymakers should deliberately design those environments to steer people toward choices that serve their own long-term interests, while preserving freedom of choice. They called this approach libertarian paternalism, and its instrument the nudge: automatic enrollment in retirement plans with an opt-out, cafeteria layouts that place fruit before fries, reminder letters that increase tax compliance.`,`The initial results were encouraging enough to launch a small revolution in governance. Nudge units sprouted in governments on several continents, and randomized trials reported real, if modest, effects: automatic enrollment dramatically raised retirement savings participation; simplified forms increased college financial-aid applications; timely text messages reduced missed medical appointments. Defenders argued that nudges offered a rare political free lunch — welfare gains without mandates, bans, or significant public expenditure. Where traditional regulation coerces, nudging merely rearranges the choice architecture, leaving the individual free to opt out. What, the defenders asked, could be objectionable about helping people do what, on reflection, they themselves want to do?`,`Critics answered: a great deal, and on two distinct fronts. The first objection concerns autonomy. Even when opting out is formally permitted, critics argue, nudges work precisely by bypassing rational deliberation — exploiting inertia, inattention, and cognitive bias rather than engaging citizens as reasoning agents. A government that steers behavior through psychological levers it would be embarrassed to defend openly, the critics contend, treats its citizens as subjects to be managed rather than as equals to be persuaded. The second objection is empirical. As the nudge literature matured, large-scale replications and meta-analyses suggested that many celebrated effects were smaller and more context-dependent than early studies implied. Default enrollment works reliably; many subtler interventions — social-norm messages, framing tweaks — show effects that shrink on replication or vary unpredictably across settings. The free lunch, it turned out, was smaller than advertised.`,`Defenders have responded to both charges without conceding the core idea. To the autonomy objection, they reply that choice architecture is unavoidable: the retirement form must have some default, the cafeteria some layout, and pretending otherwise is not neutrality but abdication. If influence is inevitable, they argue, it should be wielded transparently and in citizens’ interests rather than left to chance or to private actors with no such scruples. To the empirical objection, they concede that early enthusiasm outran the evidence but note that this is the normal lifecycle of a research program, not its refutation. Policy, they add, should follow the strongest findings — defaults, reminders, simplification — while treating flashier interventions with appropriate skepticism.`,`The debate’s current frontier concerns not whether nudges work but when their use is legitimate. Proposals for ethical guardrails have proliferated: that nudges be publicly disclosed, that they steer only toward ends citizens would endorse on reflection, and that they be tested rigorously before deployment at scale. These constraints would not satisfy the strongest critics, who see manipulation in any steering that bypasses reason. But they reflect a maturing recognition on both sides — that the psychology underlying nudging is real, that its policy effects are genuine but limited, and that the interesting question was never whether choice architecture influences us. It does. Unanswered is the institutional question beneath the guardrails: who may design the architecture of choice, under whose oversight, and with what recourse for those being steered.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p07-q1`,`rc-p07-q2`,`rc-p07-q3`,`rc-p07-q4`,`rc-p07-q5`,`rc-p07-q6`,`rc-p07-q7`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p08`,title:`The Resource Curse Reconsidered`,domain:`social-science`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`It is one of development economics’ most counterintuitive findings: countries blessed with abundant natural resources — oil, gas, diamonds, copper — have frequently grown more slowly, governed worse, and fought more civil wars than their resource-poor neighbors. Economists call this pattern the resource curse, or the paradox of plenty. Its canonical statement holds that resource wealth distorts economies and polities through several reinforcing channels. Windfall revenues inflate the currency and draw investment away from manufacturing, hollowing out the tradable sector in a syndrome known as Dutch disease. Easy money relieves governments of the need to tax citizens, weakening the bargain — taxation in exchange for representation and accountability — on which responsive institutions historically rest. And concentrated, lootable wealth raises the stakes of holding power, inviting corruption, patronage, and armed conflict over control of the prize.`,`The thesis gained influence because the examples seemed so stark. Oil-rich Nigeria and Venezuela, despite decades of petroleum revenue, experienced economic stagnation, spectacular corruption, and political turmoil. Sierra Leone’s diamonds funded a brutal civil war. Cross-country statistical studies in the 1990s appeared to confirm the pattern: controlling for other factors, resource abundance correlated with slower growth. For policymakers and international institutions, the message was sobering — that geology could be destiny, and that the discovery of oil beneath a poor country’s soil might be cause for dread rather than celebration.`,`Yet the resource curse has always had conspicuous exceptions, and its critics have grown bolder in arguing that the exceptions undermine the rule. Norway converted North Sea oil into the world’s largest sovereign wealth fund and some of its most trusted institutions. Botswana parlayed diamonds into decades of rapid growth and stable democracy. Malaysia, Chile, and Indonesia managed resource booms without succumbing to the full syndrome. Critics contend that these cases reveal the real variable: not resources but the quality of institutions that existed before the windfall arrived. Where competent bureaucracies, independent courts, and mechanisms of accountability were already in place, resource wealth became a blessing; where they were absent, it became fuel for the pathologies the curse describes. On this revisionist view, resources do not curse countries — they amplify whatever institutional trajectory a country is already on.`,`Defenders of the curse thesis have refined rather than abandoned it. They concede that the crude claim — resources always harm — is false, but argue that the probabilistic claim survives: resource wealth raises the odds of institutional decay even if it does not guarantee it. They point to evidence that windfalls actively degrade governance over time, not merely that they arrive in badly governed places — for instance, studies showing that oil discoveries are followed by measurable declines in press freedom and increases in patronage spending. The exceptions, they add, prove demanding rather than comforting: Norway and Botswana succeeded through deliberate, difficult institutional engineering — transparency rules, stabilization funds, parliamentary oversight — that most developing countries find hard to replicate under the political pressures a boom creates.`,`The practical upshot of the debate is a shift in prescription. Early curse literature counseled something close to fatalism, or at best urged countries to leave resources in the ground. The revisionist synthesis counsels institutional preparation: transparent revenue management, independent oversight bodies, and rules that smooth spending across commodity cycles — ideally constructed before the first barrel is pumped. Whether such preparation is feasible in precisely the fragile states that need it most remains an open question. For policymakers, the revisionist lesson is sequential: build the oversight institutions before the revenues arrive, because the evidence suggests that sequencing — more than the size of the windfall — determines who benefits and who is left to watch the money flow.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p08-q1`,`rc-p08-q2`,`rc-p08-q3`,`rc-p08-q4`,`rc-p08-q5`,`rc-p08-q6`,`rc-p08-q7`],estimatedMinutes:10},{id:`rc-p09`,title:`The Idea That Agriculture Was a Mistake`,domain:`social-science`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`In the 1960s, the anthropologist Marshall Sahlins advanced a claim that quietly detonated the standard story of human progress. Studying contemporary foraging peoples, Sahlins argued that hunter-gatherers were, by a certain definition, affluent: they satisfied their material wants with only a few hours of work a day, ate varied diets, and enjoyed abundant leisure. Affluence, he suggested in his 1972 essay “The Original Affluent Society,” need not mean endless production — it could mean modest wants easily met. Sahlins was not attacking agriculture directly, but his argument removed the premise on which the conventional story rested. If foragers lived well, farming’s triumph could not be explained as humanity’s escape from misery.`,`Two decades later, Jared Diamond converted Sahlins’s suggestion into an indictment. In a widely read 1987 essay, Diamond called agriculture “the worst mistake in the history of the human race.” Where Sahlins had studied living foragers, Diamond invoked the dead: bioarchaeologists were finding that the skeletons of early farmers were shorter, more malnourished, and more ravaged by disease than those of the foragers they replaced. Dependence on a few staple crops brought protein and micronutrient deficiencies; crowded settlements bred crowd diseases; and the archaeological record suggested farmers worked longer hours than foragers for a worse diet. Diamond’s essay turned scattered findings into a provocation with a single sharp edge: the thing we call progress had made its first adopters worse off.`,`In the 1980s and 1990s, the skeletal evidence became a research program. The 1984 volume Paleopathology at the Origins of Agriculture, edited by Mark Nathan Cohen and George Armelagos, assembled studies from every continent showing the same pattern at the agricultural transition: shrinking stature, dental disease, skeletal signatures of anemia and infection. But the deeper evidence base also produced the first major qualification of Diamond’s case. Critics noted that the bones documented a transitional penalty, not a permanent verdict: later agricultural societies, with better techniques, more diverse crops, and eventually public health, surpassed forager outcomes. Judging agriculture by its first millennia, they argued, was like judging industrialization by the slums of the 1840s. The debate’s center of gravity shifted from whether early farming hurt to how long the hurt lasted.`,`A second refinement answered the question Diamond’s indictment left hanging: if farming made people worse off, why did it conquer the globe? Scholars working in the tradition of Ester Boserup, who had linked population growth to agricultural intensification, supplied a demographic mechanism. Farming’s decisive advantage was yield per acre: a farmed landscape could feed orders of magnitude more people than a foraged one. Farming populations therefore expanded, displacing or absorbing foraging neighbors, and once a region’s numbers exceeded what foraging could support, there was no going back — return would have meant mass starvation. Societies were not persuaded by agriculture’s benefits, on this account; they were captured by its irreversibility. The “demographic trap” turned Diamond’s moral provocation into a structural explanation.`,`The current state of the discussion would be only partly recognizable to either Sahlins or Diamond. Few researchers now defend the strong claim that agriculture was simply a mistake, and few defend the old progress narrative either. Attention has moved to specifying trade-offs: individual welfare against collective capability, health against numbers, the well-being of the living against the possibilities of the unborn. Ancient-DNA studies have added a further complication, showing that farming often spread through the migration of farming peoples as well as the conversion of foragers — a finding that reframes the demographic trap itself as partly a story of displacement. The question Sahlins first posed has thus survived every attempt to settle it, changing shape with each generation of evidence.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p09-q1`,`rc-p09-q2`,`rc-p09-q3`,`rc-p09-q4`,`rc-p09-q5`,`rc-p09-q6`,`rc-p09-q7`,`rc-p09-q8`],estimatedMinutes:10},{id:`rc-p10`,title:`Does Language Shape Thought?`,domain:`social-science`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Do speakers of different languages think differently? The strong version of this idea — that language determines the boundaries of thought, so that what cannot be said cannot be conceived — was largely abandoned by linguists decades ago as both untestable and implausible. But a weaker, subtler version has staged a remarkable comeback. Researchers in the neo-Whorfian tradition argue that while language does not imprison thought, it habitually nudges it: the categories and distinctions a language obliges its speakers to make leave traces on memory, perception, and reasoning, even in non-linguistic tasks. Grammar, on this view, is not merely a vehicle for expressing thought but a quiet sculptor of it.`,`The most studied test case concerns color. Languages differ dramatically in how they divide the spectrum: English distinguishes blue from green, while some languages use a single term covering both, and Russian obligatorily distinguishes light blue from dark blue. In a series of influential experiments, researchers found that Russian speakers were faster than English speakers at discriminating shades that straddled the Russian light-blue/dark-blue boundary — but only when they could silently name the colors to themselves; under verbal interference, the advantage vanished. Similar effects have been reported for spatial reasoning — speakers of languages that use absolute directions (north, south) rather than relative ones (left, right) show superior dead-reckoning abilities — and for time, where speakers of languages that spatialize time vertically rather than horizontally arrange temporal sequences accordingly.`,`Skeptics urge caution, and their objections are methodological as much as theoretical. Many celebrated effects, they note, are small, fragile, and difficult to replicate — precisely the profile one expects from publication bias in an exciting research area. More fundamentally, critics question the direction of causation. That Russian speakers discriminate certain blues faster might reflect not linguistic causation but shared environment or the simple fact that people who habitually attend to a distinction get better at perceiving it, with language merely along for the ride. And even the positive findings, skeptics emphasize, demonstrate influence under narrowly controlled conditions — milliseconds of advantage in laboratory tasks — a far cry from the grand claim that language shapes worldviews.`,`Neo-Whorfians have responded by narrowing their claims in ways that make them harder to dismiss. Few now defend the proposition that language constrains what can be thought; the claim is rather that language shapes what is habitually thought — which distinctions are routinely noticed, which inferences come most readily. On this modest reading, the laboratory effects, small as they are, matter: they show that linguistic categories penetrate cognition below the level of conscious deliberation. The Russian blue effect disappears under verbal interference, proponents note, which is exactly what a language-mediated effect should do — it demonstrates the mechanism rather than debunking it.`,`Where does this leave the original question? Probably with a both/and rather than an either/or. Human cognition appears to rest on a shared universal foundation — the same perceptual machinery, the same capacity for abstract reasoning — overlaid with habitual tunings supplied by language and culture. Your native tongue does not determine what you can think, but it may influence what you notice first, remember best, and find easiest to say. That is a humbler thesis than linguistic determinism, but it is also, in a way, a more interesting one: it suggests that the languages we inherit are not just tools we use but environments we inhabit, quietly shaping the texture of mental life without our ever noticing.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p10-q1`,`rc-p10-q2`,`rc-p10-q3`,`rc-p10-q4`,`rc-p10-q5`,`rc-p10-q6`,`rc-p10-q7`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p11`,title:`Explaining Altruism: Four Accounts`,domain:`natural-science`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Few facts in nature look stranger than altruism: an organism reducing its own reproductive prospects to benefit another. Worker ants labor their whole lives without reproducing; ground squirrels sound alarm calls that draw a predator’s attention to themselves; vampire bats regurgitate blood meals to feed roost-mates who failed to feed. If natural selection favors traits that spread their bearers’ genes, self-sacrifice seems to defy the theory’s central logic. Biologists have proposed several distinct explanations, each with its own logic and its own flagship evidence. The puzzle has survived because more than one of them appears to be right — in different cases.`,`The most influential account is kin selection, formalized by W. D. Hamilton in the 1960s. Hamilton’s insight was that selection propagates genes, not individuals, and that relatives carry copies of the same genes. A gene promoting aid to kin can therefore spread even when it harms its bearer, provided the benefit to relatives, discounted by relatedness, exceeds the cost — Hamilton’s rule, rB > C. The account’s showcase is the sterile castes of ants, bees, and wasps: the most extreme altruism known occurs exactly where relatedness is highest. Alarm-calling squirrels, which warn kin more readily than strangers, fit the same template. Where beneficiaries are relatives, kin selection is usually the first hypothesis tested.`,`A second account, reciprocal altruism, explains cooperation among non-relatives. Proposed by Robert Trivers in 1971, it holds that altruism can evolve when favors are exchanged over repeated encounters, with cheaters detected and punished. The vampire bat is the textbook case: bats that fail to feed are fed by roost-mates, but donors keep score — individuals that accept blood without ever donating are eventually refused. No kinship is required, only memory, repeated interaction, and the capacity to withhold future help. Where kin selection leans on shared genes, reciprocal altruism leans on the shadow of the future: today’s cost is repaid by tomorrow’s return.`,`A third account, multilevel or group selection, revived an older idea with new mathematics. Its champions, including E. O. Wilson in a controversial 2010 paper, argue that selection operates simultaneously within and between groups: within any group, selfish individuals outcompete altruists, but groups rich in altruists outcompete groups of egoists — founding more daughter colonies, surviving crises better. When the between-group advantage dominates, altruism can evolve even among non-relatives. Critics reply that the models, properly formalized, are mathematically equivalent to kin selection — a relabeling, not a rival — and that apparent cases of low-relatedness cooperation dissolve when effective relatedness is measured carefully.`,`A fourth account locates the payoff in reputation rather than in returned favors or shared genes. Costly signaling theory, developed by Amotz Zahavi, proposes that conspicuous generosity functions as an honest advertisement of quality: only a genuinely fit individual can afford to give lavishly, so displays of altruism attract mates and allies. Arabian babblers that stand sentinel or feed others’ chicks, on this view, are not sacrificing for kin or banking future reciprocation — they are demonstrating vigor to an audience. The prediction that distinguishes this account is its audience effect: remove the onlookers, and the generosity should fade.`,`Practicing biologists increasingly treat these four as a toolkit rather than a set of rival creeds. Kin selection remains the default for aid among relatives; reciprocal altruism for stable exchange among non-kin; group selection for cases where group structure does visible causal work; costly signaling where generosity tracks an audience. The productive question has shifted from which account is true to which fits a given case — and the hardest cases, like human cooperation, may implicate all four at once. The altruism puzzle endures not because biology lacks an answer, but because it has several, and nature uses them all.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p11-q1`,`rc-p11-q2`,`rc-p11-q3`,`rc-p11-q4`,`rc-p11-q5`,`rc-p11-q6`,`rc-p11-q7`],estimatedMinutes:10},{id:`rc-p12`,title:`Rewilding and the Trophic Cascade Debate`,domain:`natural-science`,comparative:!1,topicCluster:`rewilding-wolves`,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Few ecological stories have captured the public imagination like the return of wolves to Yellowstone. Gray wolves, eradicated from the park by the 1920s, were reintroduced in 1995. Within a decade, popular accounts reported a cascade of transformations: elk, no longer browsing fearlessly, avoided exposed riverbanks; willows and aspens, released from browsing pressure, rebounded; beavers returned to the restored willow thickets; even the rivers themselves, the most enthusiastic telling claimed, changed course as stabilized banks reshaped the channels. The wolf became an emblem of a powerful ecological idea — the trophic cascade, in which a top predator’s influence ripples downward through herbivores to reshape entire landscapes — and of a bolder conservation philosophy, rewilding, which holds that restoring apex predators can heal degraded ecosystems from the top down.`,`The scientific foundation for trophic cascades is real but narrower than the popular story suggests. The concept’s strongest evidence comes from relatively simple systems — sea otters, urchins, and kelp forests, for example — where removing the predator produces dramatic, well-documented transformations. Large terrestrial mammals present a far messier picture. Yellowstone’s elk herds were simultaneously affected by drought, severe winters, hunting outside the park, and competition with a burgeoning bison population, all of which could explain declining elk numbers and recovering vegetation independently of wolves. Disentangling the wolf’s contribution from these confounding factors has proven formidably difficult, and some researchers argue that the famous "ecology of fear" — the claim that elk avoided risky areas because of wolves — rests on behavioral observations that later, more careful studies failed to replicate robustly.`,`Critics of the rewilding narrative press a further point: even where predators do reshape ecosystems, the reshaping is not always the restoration the story implies. Predators do not consult historical baselines; they eat what is available, and their effects depend on landscape, climate, and the particular assemblage of species present. Reintroducing wolves to a twenty-first-century Yellowstone — warmer, drier, and more visited than the Yellowstone of 1900 — cannot recreate the ecosystem of 1900, because that ecosystem’s other conditions no longer exist. What rewilding produces, on this view, is not a restored past but a novel ecosystem, one whose desirability must be argued for on its own terms rather than assumed from its resemblance to a historical snapshot.`,`Rewilding’s defenders do not deny these complications, but they argue that the critics set the bar impossibly high. Conservation, they note, rarely enjoys the luxury of controlled experiments; waiting for perfect causal attribution before acting means waiting forever while ecosystems continue to degrade. The Yellowstone evidence, while confounded, consistently points in the same direction — multiple independent lines of observation, from willow growth rings to beaver colony counts, align with the cascade hypothesis — and the broader pattern across rewilding projects is encouraging enough to justify continued experimentation. Moreover, defenders add, rewilding’s goals were never purely ecological: the return of an apex predator has cultural and ethical value, restoring a completeness to the landscape that matters even if every predicted cascade does not materialize on schedule.`,`The debate ultimately turns on what counts as sufficient evidence for action under uncertainty. The skeptics demand the standards of the laboratory — isolated variables, replicated results — before endorsing interventions that rearrange living communities. The advocates accept a humbler epistemology: convergent but imperfect evidence, weighed against the costs of inaction. Both sides agree that the early, triumphalist version of the Yellowstone story oversold what was known. Where they differ is on the lesson — with the irony that the argument over whether science oversold a conservation story is now itself retold, in op-eds and documentaries, with the same confident gloss both sides claim to distrust.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p12-q1`,`rc-p12-q2`,`rc-p12-q3`,`rc-p12-q4`,`rc-p12-q5`,`rc-p12-q6`],estimatedMinutes:10},{id:`rc-p13`,title:`The Faint Young Sun Paradox`,domain:`natural-science`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Four billion years ago, the Sun shone roughly thirty percent dimmer than it does today. Stellar physics leaves no room for doubt on this point: stars like the Sun brighten steadily as they age, fusing hydrogen into helium and growing hotter at their cores. Yet the geological record insists that liquid water — and, within a few hundred million years, life — was present on the early Earth. Riverbeds and sedimentary rocks nearly four billion years old testify to flowing water; isotopic signatures in ancient minerals suggest temperate conditions. The contradiction is stark: under a faint young Sun, straightforward climate calculations predict a planet locked in ice, perhaps frozen to the equator. That the early Earth was instead wet and clement is the faint young Sun paradox, and resolving it has occupied planetary scientists for half a century.`,`The most widely favored resolution invokes a stronger greenhouse effect on the early Earth. If the young atmosphere contained far higher concentrations of heat-trapping gases than today’s, the extra insulation could have compensated for the dimmer Sun. Carbon dioxide is the leading candidate: with little exposed continental rock to weather and draw CO₂ from the air, and with volcanic outgassing proceeding apace, atmospheric CO₂ may have been hundreds of times its modern level. Methane likely helped as well, produced abundantly by the methanogenic microbes thought to dominate early ecosystems. Climate models suggest that this combination could indeed have kept the early Earth above freezing — a neat solution that requires no exotic physics, only an atmosphere very different from our own.`,`The greenhouse solution, however, faces a stubborn geochemical objection. Certain ancient minerals — most notably the iron-rich formations known as paleosols, fossil soils — appear to record atmospheric CO₂ levels far lower than the strong-greenhouse models require. If those readings are correct, carbon dioxide alone cannot close the gap, and some additional warming mechanism must be invoked. Proposed supplements abound: other greenhouse gases such as ammonia or carbonyl sulfide; a lower planetary albedo from fewer reflective clouds; even a somewhat more massive young Sun that has since shed mass through powerful stellar winds, making the "faint" Sun less faint than standard models assume. Each proposal solves part of the puzzle while creating new ones, since the supplementary mechanisms must themselves be reconciled with the geological evidence.`,`A more radical possibility is that the paradox’s premise is partly wrong — that the early Earth was not, in fact, continuously clement. Some researchers argue that the geological evidence is compatible with long intervals of global or near-global glaciation punctuated by warmer episodes, a "slushball" Earth rather than a temperate one. On this view, the liquid-water signatures in the rock record sample the warm intervals, while the cold stretches left subtler traces. Life, originating perhaps in ice-covered oceans kept liquid by geothermal heat, would have endured the glaciations and flourished in the thaws. This solution trades one difficulty for another: it must explain what repeatedly rescued the planet from the ice, since a fully frozen Earth reflects so much sunlight that escaping glaciation becomes extremely difficult.`,`The paradox endures because every proposed solution is constrained by two independent bodies of evidence — the physics of stars and the chemistry of rocks — that refuse to be satisfied simultaneously without strain. Its persistence is nevertheless scientifically productive: the effort to resolve it has sharpened techniques for reading ancient atmospheres, improved models of planetary climate, and informed the search for habitable exoplanets, where the same faint-star problem recurs around the galaxy’s abundant dim red dwarfs. The next generation of atmospheric models will supply the verdict the current evidence cannot: either a self-consistent solution vindicates the decades of sharpened technique, or the paradox stands, and keeps an entire field honest about what it does not yet know.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p13-q1`,`rc-p13-q2`,`rc-p13-q3`,`rc-p13-q4`,`rc-p13-q5`,`rc-p13-q6`,`rc-p13-q7`],estimatedMinutes:10}],t=[{id:`rc-p14`,title:`Reading Statutes: Plain Meaning or Purpose?`,domain:`law`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`When judges interpret a statute, the most frequently announced rule is also the simplest: follow the text. Under the plain-meaning rule, if the language of a law is clear and unambiguous, courts must apply it as written, even when the result seems unwise. The appeal is obvious. Statutes are compromises hammered out by a legislature; letting judges rewrite them in the name of fairness or common sense risks replacing democratic lawmaking with judicial policymaking. The text, proponents insist, is the law.`,`Yet for every judge who claims the text settles the matter, another insists that words alone rarely settle anything. This second camp, often called purposivists, argues that language is inherently imprecise and that a statute is best understood as a solution to a problem. Consider a law that bars anyone who has "unlawfully killed" another person from inheriting from the victim. A grandson who murders his grandfather to collect the inheritance has plainly done what the words describe — yet no court has hesitated to deny him the inheritance, reasoning that the legislature could not have meant to reward murder. For purposivists, such cases prove that meaning depends on the mischief the law was meant to cure, not merely on dictionary definitions.`,`Textualists reply that hard cases make bad interpretive theory. The murderous heir is an extreme example, they argue, and building a whole method around extremes licenses judges to smuggle their own preferences into ordinary cases under the respectable cover of "purpose." Purpose, they point out, is not a fact waiting to be discovered; a single statute typically serves several purposes, sometimes in tension, and choosing among them is itself an act of discretion. The plain-meaning rule may occasionally produce a harsh result, but it constrains judges, gives citizens fair notice of the law, and keeps courts out of the legislature's business.`,`The debate, however, is less absolute than the rhetoric suggests. Few textualists claim that a statute should be read with no attention to context: they consult neighboring provisions, established legal usage, and the canons of construction that courts have used for centuries. And few purposivists ignore the text: even the most committed among them begin with the words and depart from them only when the text, read in context, would defeat the law's evident design. In practice, the two approaches often converge on the same outcome, differing mainly in what they emphasize — the discipline of the text or the flexibility of its aim.`,`New technology keeps reopening the quarrel. When a decades-old privacy statute written for telephone wiretaps is applied to location data from smartphones, textualists ask what the words covered at enactment, while purposivists ask what problem the legislature was trying to solve and how that problem appears today. Neither side has surrendered, and neither is likely to.`,`The deeper question remains whether any method can fully separate interpretation from judgment. The canons and dictionaries that textualists favor are themselves products of judicial choice, and the purposes that purposivists invoke must be inferred from the very texts they claim to transcend. Perhaps the honest conclusion is that the plain-meaning rule describes an aspiration — a commitment to decide cases on the basis of enacted words — rather than a formula that decides cases by itself.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p14-q1`,`rc-p14-q2`,`rc-p14-q3`,`rc-p14-q4`,`rc-p14-q5`,`rc-p14-q6`,`rc-p14-q7`,`rc-p14-q8`],estimatedMinutes:11},{id:`rc-p15`,title:`The Restorer's Dilemma`,domain:`humanities`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Every act of art restoration is also an act of interpretation. A conservator who cleans a darkened painting, fills a loss, or reattaches a flaking surface is not simply revealing the work as it was; the conservator is deciding, brushstroke by brushstroke, what the work should be. This tension sits at the heart of modern conservation: the duty to preserve the object for the future collides with the risk of overwriting the past.`,`Professional conservators have developed an ethical framework to manage the collision. Its central principle is reversibility: any treatment should, as far as possible, be undoable, so that future generations — armed with better techniques and different values — can revisit the decision. Restorers document every intervention, use materials that can be removed without harming the original, and favor minimal action. The cleaning of the Sistine Chapel ceiling in the 1980s and 1990s, which stripped away centuries of soot and overpainting to reveal unexpectedly vivid colors, was defended on exactly these grounds: the grime was not Michelangelo's work, and removing it restored the artist's own hand.`,`Critics were not persuaded. Art historians and fellow artists charged that the cleaning had destroyed something authentic — the patina of age, the subtle tonal harmonies that centuries of viewers had actually experienced, and possibly Michelangelo's own final glazes, which the restorers mistook for dirt. On this view, reversibility is a comforting fiction: once a surface is altered, no record, however meticulous, can put it back. The cleaned ceiling may be closer to the sixteenth century, the critics argued, but it is farther from every century in between, and those intervening centuries are part of the work's history too.`,`Both sides, notably, accept that some intervention is unavoidable. Paintings flake, sculptures corrode, and textiles fade; a policy of absolute non-interference would simply watch masterpieces disintegrate. The real dispute is about the burden of proof. Conservators place it on those who would leave damage untreated, arguing that neglect is itself a choice with irreversible consequences. Their critics place it on those who would intervene, arguing that every restoration gambles the irreplaceable on the taste of the present.`,`Digital technology has opened a third path. High-resolution imaging now allows restorers to produce virtual reconstructions — showing how a faded fresco might once have looked — without touching the physical object. Museums increasingly exhibit the damaged original alongside its digital restoration, letting viewers see both the work's history and its hypothesized past. Yet even this compromise involves interpretation: someone must decide which pigments to simulate and which losses to fill, and those decisions embed the same judgments the physical restorer makes.`,`Underlying the technical debate is a question of authority. Does a painting belong primarily to its maker, whose intentions the restorer claims to recover? To the public, whose heritage it has become? Or to the future, which the principle of reversibility tries — perhaps impossibly — to protect? Until that question is answered, every cleaned canvas will remain, in part, a portrait of its restorer.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p15-q1`,`rc-p15-q2`,`rc-p15-q3`,`rc-p15-q4`,`rc-p15-q5`,`rc-p15-q6`,`rc-p15-q7`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p16`,title:`Basic Income Pilots: Reading the Evidence`,domain:`social-science`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`Few policy ideas have traveled as quickly from the seminar room to the pilot program as the universal basic income — the proposal to give every resident a regular, unconditional cash payment. In the past decade, governments and philanthropies have launched dozens of experiments, from a nationwide trial in Finland to city-level pilots across North America and Africa. Advocates hail the results as vindication; skeptics see the same data and shrug. The dispute is less about the numbers than about what the numbers are allowed to prove.`,`The headline findings are by now familiar. Recipients report lower stress, better health, and greater ability to handle emergencies. Employment effects are modest: most studies find small reductions in hours worked, concentrated among students, new parents, and people between jobs, rather than a mass exodus from the labor force. Children in recipient households tend to stay in school longer, and some pilots show improvements in nutrition and mental health. For supporters, this is the core of the case: cash works, people do not squander it, and the feared collapse of work effort has not materialized.`,`Critics do not dispute the measurements; they dispute the leap from pilot to policy. A pilot is, by definition, partial and temporary. Participants know the payments will end, which changes how they behave — a student may sensibly cut back on shifts during a two-year experiment in a way she would not under a permanent program. More fundamentally, pilots are usually funded by outside grants, so they reveal nothing about the taxes that would be needed to pay for a genuine universal program, nor about how those taxes would affect the very labor markets the pilots are meant to study. Testing a lifeboat, the critics say, tells you little about building an ark.`,`There is also the question of scale. A pilot enrolling a few thousand people cannot capture economy-wide effects: if everyone received a basic income, wages, prices, and migration patterns might all shift in ways no small experiment can detect. Supporters concede the point but reverse its moral: the absence of large-scale evidence, they argue, is a reason to run larger experiments, not a reason to dismiss the idea. Dismissing basic income for lack of perfect evidence, they note, sets a standard that no major social program — public schooling, retirement pensions — could have met in advance.`,`Where both sides find common ground is on the value of pilots as design tools. Even skeptics acknowledge that the experiments have answered practical questions: monthly payments appear to work better than lump sums, unconditional cash outperforms tightly restricted vouchers in most settings, and administrative simplicity is a genuine virtue. The pilots have not settled whether societies should adopt a basic income, but they have clarified what adopting one would entail.`,`That clarification may be the experiments' most important product. The fiercest disagreements about basic income were never really about two-year trials; they are about competing visions of work, security, and the state's obligations — questions no randomized trial can answer. The evidence can discipline the debate, but it cannot end it.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p16-q1`,`rc-p16-q2`,`rc-p16-q3`,`rc-p16-q4`,`rc-p16-q5`,`rc-p16-q6`,`rc-p16-q7`,`rc-p16-q8`],estimatedMinutes:10},{id:`rc-p17`,title:`From Protection to Intervention: Coral Conservation’s Change of Course`,domain:`natural-science`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`For most of the twentieth century, coral conservation meant one thing: protection. The institutional model was the marine park — set boundaries, restrict fishing and development, and let the reef heal itself. Australia created the Great Barrier Reef Marine Park in 1975, among the world’s largest protected areas, and similar reserves multiplied across the tropics through the 1980s. The operating assumption, shared by managers and funders alike, was that reefs were resilient if left alone: remove local stresses and the ecosystem’s own recuperative powers would do the rest. Intervention in the reef’s biology was not debated; it was simply outside the institutional imagination.`,`The 1998 global mass-bleaching event broke that assumption. Unprecedented ocean heat turned vast stretches of reef ghostly white — corals expelling the symbiotic algae that feed them — and protection boundaries proved irrelevant against a warming ocean. In the United States, Congress responded with the Coral Reef Conservation Act of 2000, creating a federal program inside the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration devoted to reef resilience science. The new institutional language was telling: managers began speaking of “resilience” rather than “preservation,” a semantic shift that licensed a more active role. Monitoring networks expanded, and for the first time, public money flowed toward understanding how reefs might be helped to adapt, not merely shielded.`,`Australia’s policy evolution traced a parallel arc with higher stakes, since the Great Barrier Reef dominates the country’s conservation politics. The Reef 2050 Long-Term Sustainability Plan, adopted in 2015, committed governments to water-quality targets, crown-of-thorns starfish culling programs, and tens of millions in monitoring — still recognizably the protection playbook, writ large. But successive bleaching events in 2016 and 2017, which killed roughly half the shallow-water coral in the reef’s northern sector, convinced policymakers that protection alone could not hold the line. Funding priorities began to shift from keeping threats out to building tolerance in.`,`The institutional expression of that shift was the Reef Restoration and Adaptation Program, launched in 2020 with roughly 150 million Australian dollars in public funding. The program’s premise would have been unthinkable to the marine-park generation: that reefs should be deliberately engineered for a hotter ocean. Its research portfolio includes breeding heat-tolerant corals, manipulating the symbiotic algae that confer thermal resistance, and field trials deploying lab-reared corals onto degraded reefs. The program created new bureaucratic facts — permits for releasing bred organisms, protocols for genetic diversity in broodstocks, risk assessments for large-scale outplanting — and with them, new controversies. Traditional conservationists within the agencies warned that intervention spending could siphon support from emissions reduction and water quality, the unglamorous work on which everything else depends.`,`The funding shift also exposed a governance gap. Marine parks had clear legal authority: they regulated what people could do inside a boundary. Active restoration raised questions no statute had anticipated — who authorizes the release of lab-bred corals into the wild, who bears liability if an introduced strain proves invasive or disease-prone, and how neighboring jurisdictions coordinate when larvae drift across boundaries. Regulators improvised with research permits and case-by-case approvals, but the mismatch between twentieth-century conservation law and twenty-first-century intervention science remains unresolved.`,`The result is a hybrid institutional regime that would puzzle the founders of the marine-park era. Protection remains the base layer — no serious proposal abandons water quality or fishing limits — but a growing overlay of intervention programs now treats the reef as something to be managed at the genetic level. Whether that overlay grows into the dominant paradigm or remains an emergency supplement will depend less on laboratory results than on political decisions about risk, money, and authority that have barely begun. The reefs of the next century will reflect those decisions as surely as they reflect the temperature of the sea.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p17-q1`,`rc-p17-q2`,`rc-p17-q3`,`rc-p17-q4`,`rc-p17-q5`,`rc-p17-q6`,`rc-p17-q7`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p18`,title:`The Fall and Reinvention of Eyewitness Testimony`,domain:`law`,comparative:!1,topicCluster:`eyewitness-id`,parts:[{paragraphs:[`For most of the twentieth century, the confident eyewitness was the prosecution's crown jewel. Juries found a witness who pointed at the defendant and declared "I'm sure" nearly irresistible, and appellate courts rarely second-guessed them. The Supreme Court's framework, set in Manson v. Brathwaite (1977), reflected that trust: even an identification produced by a suggestive procedure — a lineup in which the suspect conspicuously stood out — could be admitted so long as it seemed "reliable" under a totality of the circumstances. And among the circumstances courts were told to weigh was the witness's own certainty. Confidence, in other words, was treated as evidence of accuracy.`,`The crack in that edifice came from two directions at once. Beginning in the 1990s, DNA testing began freeing prisoners whose convictions had rested on eyewitness testimony; in the majority of the first several hundred such exonerations studied, mistaken identification had played a central role. Psychological research explained them. Memory, the experiments showed, is not a recording but a reconstruction — and reconstruction fails at predictable points. So-called estimator variables lie beyond the justice system's control: stress, brief exposure, a weapon drawing attention away from faces, and the well-documented difficulty of identifying members of other races. System variables, by contrast, are the system's own doing: how lineups are constructed and administered, what witnesses are told beforehand, and what they are told afterward. The cruelest finding concerned confidence itself. A witness's certainty swells with confirming feedback — "good, you picked our suspect" — which meant the Manson factor of certainty was measuring the procedure as much as the memory.`,`In 2011, the New Jersey Supreme Court did what Manson had not. In State v. Henderson, the court held extensive hearings on the science and rebuilt the framework around it: when the defense shows some evidence that a procedure was suggestive, courts must examine both estimator and system variables at a pretrial hearing, and juries receive instructions explaining how memory can fail. Other states followed with their own versions, and police departments began adopting the laboratory's prescriptions — double-blind administration, in which the officer running the lineup does not know who the suspect is; sequential presentation of faces one at a time; unbiased instructions warning that the perpetrator may not be present; and the recording of a confidence statement before any feedback can inflate it. Researchers caution that sequential lineups trade fewer false picks for somewhat fewer correct ones, a reminder that reform reallocates error rather than abolishing it.`,`The new regime has not gone uncontested. Police organizations warn of training costs and administrative burdens, particularly for small departments, and most states still apply Manson's totality test unchanged. Courts remain divided over expert testimony on eyewitness fallibility: some admit it as necessary context for jurors, others exclude it as invading the jury's province. And estimator variables set a hard ceiling on what procedure can achieve — no lineup protocol repairs a fleeting, cross-racial glimpse under extreme stress. Reform, its architects concede, can only police the system's own contributions to error.`,`The direction of travel, however, is toward a humbler evidentiary role for identification: one fallible trace among others, prized when corroborated and discounted when standing alone. A caution is in order about how far the Henderson model can travel on science alone: the research establishes that certainty is manufactured, not that any particular reform reliably unmanufactures it in the courtroom. Whether the next generation of wrongful convictions is smaller than the last will depend on reforms tested where they matter — in trials, not laboratories.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p18-q1`,`rc-p18-q2`,`rc-p18-q3`,`rc-p18-q4`,`rc-p18-q5`,`rc-p18-q6`],estimatedMinutes:9},{id:`rc-p19`,title:`Whose Heritage? The Repatriation Debate`,domain:`humanities`,comparative:!1,parts:[{paragraphs:[`In storerooms beneath the world's great museums sit objects that do not quite belong to the institutions holding them: sculptures removed from temples, manuscripts taken in wartime, ceremonial masks collected under colonial rule. A growing movement demands their return to the communities and nations from which they came. Museum directors counter that these collections serve all of humanity. The repatriation debate is often framed as a legal dispute over ownership, but at bottom it is a dispute about identity — about who gets to tell the story of a culture's past.`,`The case for return begins with context. An artifact ripped from its setting, advocates argue, is a sentence torn from a paragraph: a Benin bronze in a London gallery may be beautifully displayed, but it cannot speak as it would in the palace courtyard for which it was cast. Beyond aesthetics lies history. Many objects entered Western collections through conquest, coercion, or transactions that would not pass scrutiny today, and their continued display, critics say, silently normalizes that history. Returning them is therefore not merely generosity; it is a belated acknowledgment that the original taking was wrong.`,`The great "universal" museums answer with a different vision. Institutions like the British Museum or the Louvre present themselves as custodians of a shared human heritage, where a visitor can encounter the art of every civilization under one roof. They argue that they preserve objects with resources — climate-controlled storage, expert conservation, security — that many source institutions cannot match, and that dispersing collections would impoverish the cross-cultural encounters these museums make possible. Some directors add a slippery-slope warning: if every object with a contested past must go, few galleries would have anything left to show.`,`Experience has complicated both positions. The returns that have occurred — a Berlin museum's restitution of bronze plaques to Nigeria, the repatriation of ancestral remains to Indigenous communities — have generally been celebrated rather than mourned, and several receiving institutions have built impressive new museums to house returned works, undercutting the claim that they cannot care for them. Conversely, some source governments have themselves been accused of neglecting heritage or using repatriated treasures as political props, lending weight to the universalists' cautions.`,`A middle path is emerging in practice if not in principle. Long-term loans, shared stewardship agreements, and joint exhibitions allow objects to return home while remaining accessible to global audiences. Digital archives make collections visible everywhere regardless of where the physical object resides. These arrangements concede something to each side: that cultural context matters, and that broad access matters too.`,`What remains unresolved is the criterion. Everyone agrees that objects looted in living memory by identifiable thieves should go back; almost no one thinks every antiquity that crossed a border centuries ago must. Between those poles lies the vast majority of contested holdings, and there the debate continues, now conducted less through grand principle than through case-by-case negotiation — a practical settlement the theory has yet to catch up with.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-p19-q1`,`rc-p19-q2`,`rc-p19-q3`,`rc-p19-q4`,`rc-p19-q5`,`rc-p19-q6`,`rc-p19-q7`,`rc-p19-q8`],estimatedMinutes:10}],n=[{id:`rc-c01`,title:`Algorithms on the Hiring Committee`,domain:`social-science`,comparative:!0,parts:[{label:`Passage A`,paragraphs:[`The hiring process is one of the last bastions of pure subjectivity in corporate life. Resumes are skimmed in seconds, interviews reward charm over competence, and study after study shows that candidates with identical qualifications receive different treatment based on their names, their schools, or the interviewer's mood. Automated screening tools promise to replace this lottery with something fairer: algorithms that evaluate every applicant against the same criteria, ignore irrelevant personal details, and learn from data rather than gut feeling.`,`The evidence in favor of automation is substantial. Properly designed systems can be audited in ways human judgment cannot — every decision is logged, every criterion is explicit, and disparate outcomes can be detected and corrected. Several large employers report that after adopting structured algorithmic screening, the diversity of their shortlists improved markedly. Moreover, unlike a biased manager who can hide behind vague impressions, a biased algorithm leaves a paper trail; regulators and researchers can test it, challenge it, and demand fixes.`,`None of this means algorithms are neutral by nature. It means they are improvable in a way that human prejudice is not. A company cannot easily rewire the intuitions of ten thousand hiring managers, but it can retrain a model, adjust its thresholds, and publish the results. The question is not whether automated hiring is perfect — it is whether it is better than the flawed, opaque, and demonstrably discriminatory system it replaces. What matters is the direction of travel: away from untestable hunches and toward decisions that can be inspected, debated, and steadily improved.`]},{label:`Passage B`,paragraphs:[`Proponents of algorithmic hiring describe a future of objectivity, but the systems being deployed today look less like impartial judges than like efficient laundries for old biases. An algorithm trained on a company's historical hiring data learns exactly what that company has always done: favor graduates of certain universities, penalize employment gaps, reward the vocabulary of the already-employed. The discrimination does not disappear; it is simply expressed in mathematics, which makes it harder to recognize and easier to defend.`,`The much-vaunted auditability of these systems is largely theoretical. Most commercial hiring tools are proprietary black boxes, and vendors routinely resist independent testing on grounds of trade secrecy. Even when audits occur, they typically measure only narrow statistical parity while missing the deeper question of whether the criteria themselves are fair. An algorithm that screens out caregivers because past "high performers" worked uninterrupted eighty-hour weeks is not removing bias from hiring; it is automating a workplace culture that many consider part of the problem.`,`There is also an accountability gap with no human equivalent. When a manager discriminates, the victim can at least identify the decision-maker and appeal to conscience, policy, or law. When an algorithm rejects an applicant, there is often no explanation beyond a score, and no one within the company fully understands how the score was produced. Efficiency is a genuine value, but a hiring process that cannot explain its own rejections has sacrificed something essential about fair treatment: the right to be judged by reasons, not by correlations. Until hiring systems can meet that standard, their gains in efficiency remain morally incomplete.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-c01-q1`,`rc-c01-q2`,`rc-c01-q3`,`rc-c01-q4`,`rc-c01-q5`,`rc-c01-q6`,`rc-c01-q7`],estimatedMinutes:12},{id:`rc-c02`,title:`The Reintroduction Debate`,domain:`natural-science`,comparative:!0,topicCluster:`species-reintroduction`,parts:[{label:`Passage A`,paragraphs:[`In 1987, wildlife biologists captured the last 22 California condors flying free and carried them into captivity. The decision felt like an admission of defeat: the birds were dying of lead poisoning, ingesting fragments of spent ammunition from the carcasses they scavenged, and the landscape itself seemed to have turned against them. With the wild population at zero, captive breeding began from those 22 survivors. Four years later the first captive-bred condors were released; today more than 500 condors exist, with over 300 soaring wild over California, Arizona, Utah, and Baja California.`,`Proponents draw a general lesson from the condor: extinction is not always inevitable, even at the brink. When the threat is identified and removed — here, lead, through regulation and hunters voluntarily switching ammunition — intensive intervention can reverse a decline that looked terminal. Captive breeding, once dismissed as a zoo exercise, becomes a bridge back to the wild. The choice, on this view, is rarely between pristine wilderness and total loss; it is between managed recovery and managed extinction.`,`None of this was cheap or simple. The program has cost tens of millions of dollars, demands perpetual monitoring of released birds, and depends on hunters accepting non-lead ammunition — an ask that has met sustained political resistance. But the alternative was a sky permanently without condors. The question is whether conservation should be judged by its price tag or by what it preserves: a species pulled back from 22 individuals is not a curiosity but proof that determined intervention works.`]},{label:`Passage B`,paragraphs:[`The Yellowstone wolf story is a wonderful narrative, and like many wonderful narratives it has outrun its evidence. Recent research suggests that the celebrated trophic cascade was overstated: elk numbers were already declining before the wolves arrived, climate shifts confounded the vegetation data, and some of the attributed recoveries occurred in areas the wolves rarely visit. The wolf's ecological role is real but contingent — powerful in some landscapes, marginal in others — and policy built on the grandest version of the story rests on shaky ground.`,`More importantly, the costs of reintroduction are neither trivial nor equitably shared. Ranchers near wolf territories face predation losses, constant vigilance, and the stress of defending herds through calving season; compensation programs are chronically underfunded and pay only for confirmed kills, a fraction of actual losses. These burdens fall on rural communities that see little of the tourism revenue wolves generate for gateway towns. Conservation that asks one group to subsidize everyone else's wilderness ideal is not obviously just, whatever its ecological merits.`,`None of this means predators have no place in modern landscapes. It means reintroduction should be treated as a site-specific management decision — weighed against local conditions, honest about uncertainties, and negotiated with the people who will live alongside the consequences — rather than as a moral crusade to undo the past. Ecology deserves better than mythology, and so do the communities asked to bear its costs. Sound policy, like sound science, begins by taking those lived costs seriously rather than explaining them away.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-c02-q1`,`rc-c02-q2`,`rc-c02-q3`,`rc-c02-q4`,`rc-c02-q5`,`rc-c02-q6`,`rc-c02-q7`],estimatedMinutes:12},{id:`rc-c03`,title:`Saving Old Streets`,domain:`humanities`,comparative:!0,parts:[{label:`Passage A`,paragraphs:[`Historic preservation is usually defended in the language of beauty and memory, but its strongest justification may be economic. Designated historic districts consistently outperform comparable neighborhoods on the measures cities care about: property values rise, tourism spending grows, and small businesses — the cafes, galleries, and workshops that thrive on foot traffic and character — cluster in old buildings that new construction cannot replicate cheaply. A restored main street is not a museum piece; it is a productive asset.`,`The mechanism is straightforward. Distinctive older districts attract visitors and residents willing to pay a premium for authenticity, and that premium funds further rehabilitation in a virtuous cycle. Studies of heritage tourism show that travelers seek out historic character and stay longer — and spend more — where they find it. Meanwhile, rehabilitating existing buildings is often cheaper per square foot than new construction and keeps money circulating locally through skilled trades rather than exporting it to distant developers.`,`Critics warn that preservation freezes cities in amber and drives up housing costs by restricting supply. But thoughtful preservation does not prohibit growth; it channels it, protecting the irreplaceable core while directing density to appropriate sites. A city that demolishes its distinctive fabric for generic development is not becoming more affordable — it is becoming more interchangeable, and sacrificing the very qualities that made people want to live there. Character, in the end, is infrastructure of another kind: it compounds over decades while generic development depreciates, and cities that understand this build wealth that lasts.`]},{label:`Passage B`,paragraphs:[`Walk through a historic district and you are walking through a community's autobiography. The corner storefront where immigrants opened their first businesses, the church that anchored a neighborhood through hard decades, the modest rowhouses that housed generations of working families — these places carry meanings no plaque can fully capture. Preservation matters, on this view, not because old buildings are profitable but because they are irreplaceable vessels of collective memory. A city that erases them erases the evidence of who its people were.`,`This argument reaches communities that the economic case never touches. For neighborhoods whose histories include displacement, discrimination, or struggle, preserving the physical traces of that history is an act of recognition: it says these lives counted and this place mattered. The value here cannot be measured in tourist dollars. Indeed, reducing preservation to its return on investment subtly concedes that anything unprofitable may be demolished — a principle that would have erased most of what we now treasure.`,`None of this denies that preservation has practical benefits; thriving historic districts do attract investment, and no one mourns that. The point is one of priority. We should save old streets first because they embody who we are, and welcome the prosperity that follows as a fortunate consequence — not the other way around. A preservation movement that must justify itself on a balance sheet has already lost the deeper argument. Memory is not a luxury good but the precondition for a community knowing itself, and no balance sheet can price what a neighborhood remembers.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-c03-q1`,`rc-c03-q2`,`rc-c03-q3`,`rc-c03-q4`,`rc-c03-q5`,`rc-c03-q6`],estimatedMinutes:11},{id:`rc-c04`,title:`Cleaning the Sky: Direct Air Capture and Its Critics`,domain:`natural-science`,comparative:!0,parts:[{label:`Passage A`,paragraphs:[`The arithmetic of climate change has grown unforgiving. Even if every nation met its emissions pledges — and most are not on track — the world would still overshoot the temperature targets scientists consider safe. Some emissions, from aviation, shipping, cement, and agriculture, have no scalable clean alternative on the horizon. This is the gap that direct air capture (DAC) is designed to fill: machines that pull carbon dioxide straight from the atmosphere, allowing it to be stored underground permanently. The technology is expensive today, but so were solar panels forty years ago.`,`Critics call DAC a distraction, yet the models that chart a path to net-zero emissions rely on large-scale carbon removal in virtually every scenario. Planting trees helps, but forests burn, and there is simply not enough land to absorb the residual emissions of a modern economy. DAC's advantages are physical, not ideological: it requires little land, its storage can be measured and verified, and it can in principle be sited wherever clean energy and geology are favorable. Dismissing it means betting the climate on the hope that the hardest emissions turn out to be easy.`,`None of this argues against cutting emissions first — every ton not emitted is cheaper than a ton captured later. But mitigation and removal are complements, not competitors. Refusing to develop carbon removal until emissions reach zero is like refusing to fund hospitals until disease is eradicated. We will need both, and the longer we delay the second, the more expensive the first becomes. Building both at once is not extravagance; it is the prudence the arithmetic demands.`]},{label:`Passage B`,paragraphs:[`Direct air capture is an engineering marvel in search of a sensible role. Current facilities capture carbon at costs measured in hundreds of dollars per ton while consuming enormous amounts of energy — energy that, in most grids, still comes partly from fossil fuels, partially defeating the purpose. At the scale required to matter for the climate, DAC would demand an industrial buildout comparable to the entire oil and gas industry, constructed in a fraction of the time, to clean up after emissions we could have avoided in the first place.`,`The deeper objection is about incentives. Every dollar and every political promise devoted to future carbon removal is a dollar and a promise not devoted to the proven, available work of cutting emissions now: deploying renewables, electrifying transport, insulating buildings. Fossil fuel companies have been quick to embrace DAC precisely because it offers a story in which their product can continue indefinitely, with the cleanup handled later by someone else's machines. Economists call this moral hazard; the rest of us might call it an excuse.`,`This is not an argument that carbon removal research should stop — for truly unavoidable emissions, some removal capacity will eventually be needed. It is an argument about sequence and scale. Mitigation first, at full speed, with removal developed as a backstop rather than a centerpiece. A climate strategy that leads with expensive machines to scrub the sky while emissions keep rising has confused the cleanup crew with the fire department. Prevention first, cleanup second: that sequence is the whole of a serious climate strategy.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-c04-q1`,`rc-c04-q2`,`rc-c04-q3`,`rc-c04-q4`,`rc-c04-q5`,`rc-c04-q6`,`rc-c04-q7`],estimatedMinutes:12},{id:`rc-c05`,title:`How Children Learn a Second Language at School`,domain:`social-science`,comparative:!0,parts:[{label:`Passage A`,paragraphs:[`Children learn languages the way they learn everything else: through massive, meaningful exposure. This simple observation is the foundation of immersion education, in which students are taught mathematics, science, and history entirely in the target language from the first day of school. The classroom becomes a linguistic deep end, and students — like young swimmers — learn fastest when they cannot touch the bottom. Study after study finds that immersion students match or exceed their peers in subject-matter learning while acquiring dramatically stronger second-language skills.`,`The mechanism is time on task. A student who hears and uses the new language for six hours a day accumulates in one school year the exposure that a traditional forty-five-minute language class would take nearly a decade to provide. Concerns that immersion harms first-language development have not survived scrutiny: follow-up studies consistently show immersion students performing at grade level in their native language by late elementary school, with the temporary lag of the early years fully closed.`,`What immersion demands, above all, is commitment. Programs that dilute the model — carving out large blocks of native-language instruction or allowing teachers to translate difficult material — see correspondingly diluted results. Language acquisition rewards intensity, and half-measures produce half-speakers. For schools serious about bilingualism, the evidence points in one direction: maximize exposure, trust the process, and resist the temptation to make things easier in ways that make them slower. Schools that commit fully give students the one resource language learning cannot do without: sustained, daily hours in the language itself.`]},{label:`Passage B`,paragraphs:[`Immersion advocates tell a compelling story about exposure, but they overlook what actually transfers between languages. Literacy is not language-specific: a child who learns to decode text, follow an argument, and organize an essay in her first language carries those skills into her second. This is the foundation of developmental bilingual programs, which build academic proficiency in the home language while systematically adding the second. The result is students who are literate in two languages rather than semi-proficient in one and shaky in the other.`,`The research on transfer is robust. Students with strong first-language literacy acquire second-language academic skills faster, not slower, than peers whose home language was neglected — a finding that holds across dozens of studies and multiple language pairs. The early "lag" that immersion researchers celebrate as temporary is, for many children, not temporary at all: students pulled from home-language support too early disproportionately populate remedial tracks, suggesting that what looked like a shortcut was actually a detour.`,`None of this denies the value of rich second-language exposure; developmental programs are emphatically bilingual, not monolingual. The disagreement is about sequencing, not about goals. Build the foundation first — strong literacy, confident academic identity, skills that transfer — and the second language rises faster on top of it. Tearing down the foundation to build the second story sooner is impatience masquerading as pedagogy. Strong foundations are not delays but the very thing that makes later speed possible, and the transfer research proves it term after term in classrooms everywhere.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-c05-q1`,`rc-c05-q2`,`rc-c05-q3`,`rc-c05-q4`,`rc-c05-q5`,`rc-c05-q6`,`rc-c05-q7`],estimatedMinutes:12},{id:`rc-c06`,title:`Who Owns What the Machine Learned?`,domain:`law`,comparative:!0,parts:[{label:`Passage A`,paragraphs:[`Every major artificial intelligence model was trained on vast quantities of copyrighted text and images, and a wave of lawsuits now asks whether that training was lawful. The answer, under existing doctrine, should be yes: training is a textbook case of fair use. Fair use protects uses that transform the original into something new, and a model that digests millions of works to learn statistical patterns — grammar, style, the relationship between images and captions — produces nothing that substitutes for any particular work it was trained on.`,`The alternative is unworkable. Requiring licenses for every work in a training corpus of billions of items would not compensate authors meaningfully — the per-work value of training data approaches zero — but it would entrench the largest technology companies, the only ones able to negotiate such licenses at scale. Copyright was designed to promote the progress of knowledge, not to give every author a veto over new technologies that learn from published works the way human readers do.`,`None of this leaves creators without protection. If a model reproduces substantial portions of a specific work in its outputs, that output can infringe just as a human-authored copy would. The line copyright has always drawn — between learning from works and copying them — applies cleanly here. What fair use has always permitted for the student with a library card, it should permit for the machine with a dataset. To hold otherwise would let copyright obstruct the very progress in knowledge it was written to promote.`]},{label:`Passage B`,paragraphs:[`The fair-use defense of AI training rests on a sleight of hand: it treats the ingestion of millions of copyrighted works as if it were a student reading in a library. But a student reading a book does not produce a commercial product that competes with the book's author; an AI company training on an author's entire oeuvre to build a machine that generates competing text does. The scale and purpose of the use are fundamentally different, and copyright law has always treated commercial exploitation of others' creative labor as the core case for requiring permission.`,`Nor is licensing the impossibility its critics claim. Collective licensing organizations already manage rights for millions of musical works, distributing royalties to songwriters every time a song is played; a similar regime for training data is administratively feasible and would direct real money to creators. The claim that per-work values are too small to matter ignores how aggregation works: small payments across billions of uses built the entire recorded-music economy.`,`Both sides of this dispute, notably, accept the same starting premise: that copyright law — with its familiar machinery of exclusive rights, exceptions, and remedies — is the proper framework for deciding the question. No one here proposes scrapping copyright for AI or inventing an entirely new regime. The fight is over how the existing framework applies: whether mass ingestion for model training is the kind of transformative learning fair use was meant to shelter, or the kind of uncompensated commercial exploitation it was meant to prevent.`]}],questionIds:[`rc-c06-q1`,`rc-c06-q2`,`rc-c06-q3`,`rc-c06-q4`,`rc-c06-q5`,`rc-c06-q6`,`rc-c06-q7`,`rc-c06-q8`],estimatedMinutes:12}],r=[...t,...n];export{e as n,r as t};